{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And an earthen vessel in which it was boiled shall be broken.</b> That is, in a sacred place, just as the washing is in a sacred place." "<b>And if it was boiled in a copper vessel, it shall be scoured and rinsed with water.</b> This too refers to the sacred place mentioned earlier, for everything was in a sacred place: the washing, the breaking, and the rinsing. Rashi, of blessed memory, explained: ישבר — because the absorption absorbed there becomes נותר. ומורק — in the sense of women's cosmetics. ומורק ושוטף — to expel its absorption; but an earthen vessel — Scripture teaches you here that it never departs from its absorption, end quote." "<b>Here</b> Scripture has informed us that a copper vessel — and the same applies to all other types of metal in which a forbidden substance was boiled — its ruling is by Hag'alah (purging), and mere rinsing does not suffice for them. But an earthen vessel in which a forbidden substance was boiled has no remedy except by breaking, and Hag'alah does not suffice for it. And it is well known that water has three powers: cold water, lukewarm water, and boiling water. Cold water: if the forbidden substance was placed in it, its kosherization is to rinse the vessels in cold water. Lukewarm water: if the forbidden substance was placed in it, its kosherization is not by rinsing in cold water but by rinsing in lukewarm water. Boiling water: if the forbidden substance was placed in it, a vessel's kosherization is neither in cold water nor in lukewarm water but in boiling water, because a vessel into which the forbidden thing entered does not expel it except in the manner of its absorption; and this is what they said regarding the prohibition of leaven vessels on Passover: utensils used by means of cold water, such as cups, small pitchers, and flasks — one rinses them and immerses them and they are pure. Utensils used by means of hot water, such as pots, kettles, and water-heaters — one purges them and immerses them and they are pure. Utensils used by means of fire, such as spits and grates — one whitens them (by fire) and immerses them and they are pure. From this you learn that the prohibition is not expelled from the vessel except in the manner in which it was absorbed. And the fact that it says ישבר and did not say תשבר as it said תכבס — this is because the language of breaking means that one breaks the vessel into two or three pieces, so that it is no longer fit for that task for which it was originally suitable; had it said תשבר, it would have been understood from this to break it into very small pieces, to the point that a person could not use any one of the broken pieces for anything in the world — for example (Psalms 48:7): ״תשבר אניות תרשיש״; (Judges 7:20): ״וישברו הכדים״. But Scripture intended only to say that one should break it a light breaking, sufficient that the vessel is voided of its proper use. And this is what is written: ״כל אשר בתוכו יטמא ואותו תשברו״ — and it did not say תשברו; and as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: once broken, they are pure — meaning that it has already left the category of a vessel, and it is permitted to use one of its pieces; and similarly (Exodus 12:46): ״ועצם לא תשברו בו״ — not even a light breaking, for this is the way of free-born people, not to deal with bones. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted that the shards would be absorbed into the ground of the Courtyard, for otherwise the entire Courtyard would have filled up with shards; and this was one of the eighteen miracles that were performed in the Temple, and these are them: no woman ever miscarried from the smell of the sacred meat; and the sacred meat never turned rancid; and no seminal emission ever befell the High Priest on the Day of Atonement; and no fly was ever seen in the Slaughterhouse; and no disqualification was ever found in the Omer, the Two Loaves, or the Bread of the Presence — meaning that no disqualification of impurity ever occurred in them, and these three are counted as one; and rains never extinguished the fire of the woodpile; and the wind never prevailed over the column of smoke — meaning the smoke of the fire of an ordinary person, in the manner of what is written (Leviticus 1:7): ״ונתנו בני אהרן הכהן אש על המזבח״ — for even though fire descends from heaven, it is a commandment to bring from an ordinary person, since the fire above produces no smoke, as they said in tractate Yoma: five things were said about the fire of the woodpile: crouching like a lion, clear as the sun, it has substance, it consumes moist and dry, and it produces no smoke. Crouching like a lion — but did not Rabbi Ḥanina the Deputy High Priest say: I saw it crouching like a dog? No contradiction: here in the First Temple, here in the Second Temple. People stood crowded yet bowed with room; a snake or scorpion never caused harm in Jerusalem; and no person ever said to his fellow, 'The place is too cramped for me to stay overnight in Jerusalem.' These are ten miracles that our Rabbis, of blessed memory, enumerated together in one place in tractate Avot. There remain eight, and these are them: earthen vessels in which a sin-offering was boiled — and the shards were absorbed into the ground. The altar of burnt-offering, upon which the fire was continual, was overlaid with copper the thickness of a dinar, and it was made of shittim wood, and the copper did not melt and the wood did not burn. The lamps of the Menorah: in each and every lamp there was oil in the measure of its fellows, and never did one of them go out before dawn; and if the night was short, it left no oil over; and if the night was long, it lacked none. The middle lamp had no oil except the measure of the other lamps, and from it one would light all the others and with it one would finish. A thread of techelet they would hang at the entrance of the Hekhal on the Day of Atonement, and by evening it would turn white. The scapegoat that was sent to Azazel: before it reached halfway up the mountain, not a single limb remained whole in it." "<b>And I saw</b> in the Midrash of Tanḥuma, at the beginning of the section ואתה תצוה: once they lit the Menorah, the entire courtyards of Jerusalem would use its light — therefore it is said (Exodus 27:20): ״ויקחו אליך שמן זית זך כתית למאור״. And from the time Solomon built the Temple, he planted in it all kinds of golden fruit trees, and they would produce fruit in their seasons — so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted in tractate Yoma at the end of the first chapter. And furthermore there: the Bread of the Presence retained its warmth inside it for eight days, as it is said (I Samuel 21:7): ״לשום לחם חם ביום הלקחו״. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi said: a great miracle was performed with the Bread of the Presence: it was removed as warm as when it was set down, as it is said: ״לשום לחם חם״ etc. — they would remove it warm just as they would set it down warm. And they raise the difficulty: is there no more? But did Levi not say: the place of the Ark is not subject to measurement? And did Samuel not say: the cherubs stood by miracle? And they answer: he counts miracles of creation; he does not count miracles of the Ark."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {3 "The difficulty and resolution at the end cite talmudic passages (Yoma 21a/b and parallels); the distinction ניסי דבראי / ניסי דגואי is translated literally as 'miracles of creation' and 'miracles of the Ark' following the Hebrew, though the reading may reflect a textual variant; the sense is debated in the sources."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:00.389909437Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}